#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054516 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebeb4e5a-adbe-5a94-b05d-61933a687892" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Line Using Two Points"}] :question/id #uuid "4aad1578-4134-5f25-88cb-8ab4631257ee" :question/math-academy-id "e-984" :question/problem "Sketch the graph of $y=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+3$." :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\nIt is often easiest to pick one of the points to have an $x$-coordinate of $x=0$. So, for the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0$. Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -\\frac{1}{2}x + 3 \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}(0) + 3 \\\\\n&= 0 + 3 \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,3)$.\n\nFor the second point, let's choose $x=4$. Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -\\frac{1}{2}x + 3 \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}(4) + 3 \\\\\n&=-2 + 3 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(4,1)$.\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+3\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/95/Source/Images/59583e209f4bf6bf53ab3450bb0b028e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055278 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebeb4e5a-adbe-5a94-b05d-61933a687892" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching a Line Using Two Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0a3946e-4d98-5306-b4b2-f900a60e9abf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/95/Source/Images/q-54286-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053341} {:answer/id #uuid "f18a5b0b-4150-51bf-ae4a-e1621ffc7785" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/95/Source/Images/q-54286-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186053342} {:answer/id #uuid "15e16ea3-e05e-53e9-8ea7-ae8c5c48c1